# === This file is BOTH .gitignore and .dockerignore ===
# .dockerignore is a symlink to this file, by project decision, so there is one list to maintain
# instead of two that drift. Two consequences worth knowing before editing:
#   1. Anything added here disappears from git AND from every image build context. Never add a path
#      an image needs (config/, engine/, servers/, modules/) or a doc that should stay tracked.
#   2. Paths are anchored with a leading slash where root-only is meant. Docker treats every pattern
#      as relative to the context root anyway; git would otherwise match the name at any depth, and
#      an unanchored `package.json` would also drop the per-project manifests the images build from.

# === Secrets & local config (never commit) ===
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
secrets/

# === Local-only, kept off every remote by project decision ===
# The agent contract, the working agreement and the safety rules stay on the authoring host: they
# describe how this lab is operated, which is not something the published code needs to carry.
CLAUDE.md
/AGENTS.md
/SECURITY.md

# === Local-only tooling config (useful here, not part of the shipped range) ===
/.editorconfig
/package.json
/package-lock.json

# === Operator-side tooling and lab inventory (separate PRIVATE repo, checked out here) ===
# ops/ holds the capture rig that produces the README clip, raw recordings, and the lab host
# inventory. None of it is part of the reproduce-to-detect loop, and some of it names real
# infrastructure, so it lives in its own repo rather than this one. Deliberately NOT a submodule: a
# submodule would record a pointer nobody outside the lab can clone.
ops/

# === Version control (a no-op for git, keeps history out of every build context) ===
.git/

# === Node / build ===
node_modules/
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# === Python tooling cache (the offline helpers under engine/cli/) ===
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ruff_cache/

# === Evidence ===
# The sanitised captures under modules/*/evidence/*.txt ARE committed: they are the reproduction
# proof, and since `range verify` writes them, they are a product of a real run. Raw artefacts are
# not: they are large, they are binary, and they routinely carry real addresses.
*.pcap
*.har
*.mp4
*.webm
modules/*/evidence/raw/
modules/*/evidence/*.png
modules/*/evidence/*.jpg
modules/*/evidence/*.jpeg

# Raw screen captures live next to the capture rig in the private ops/ checkout (already ignored
# above). Only the finished, frame-reviewed clip is committed, under the module it belongs to
# (see media/README.md for the publication rule).

# === OS / editor cruft ===
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
